<p>OH. I had no idea that one didn't stick around. Explains why this is familiar! <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">(God, I'm bad at this.)</span></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Ramblings&nbsp;as&nbsp;I&nbsp;read</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Now that I can approach this on its own merits&#8230; I still feel like the Department of Abnormalities isn't a thing that really needs more than one article? Like, seriously, guys, c'mon? People complained about dado saturation, after all; and now I realize they basically all take the form of &quot;building full of cells&quot;.</p>
<p>Also, holy shit, this is long.</p>
<p>You've got a really great hook with that redaction immediately following the first explanation of -2, though. Loving &quot;The Pentagram&quot;.</p>
<p>Oh my god, this seance interview is utter bugnutty perfection. I love that the interviewer is just completely out of his depth. This guy was nuts! And&#8230; it sounds like he's not actually dead? It seemed at first like he might have died in the crash, but he went up as a ghost? (Yeah, that becomes clear by the end. What.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It will be a Russian who takes the first step across the moon's surface. History will not care whether he was alive or dead.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the stuff dreams are made of. These letters are excellent.</p>
<p>Wow, that was fucked up. I can absolutely ignore my issues with the single instance of the DoA and upvote.</p>
</div>
